<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uruguayan Civil War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1839">1839</a>–<a href="/years/1851">1851</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Guerra Grande</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Uruguayan Civil War, known as the Guerra Grande, was fought from 1839 to 1851 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorados</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fructuoso-rivera">Fructuoso Rivera</a> and the Blancos of Manuel Oribe. Oribe was backed by the Argentine ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-manuel-de-rosas">Juan Manuel de Rosas</a>, while the Colorados had support at times from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and Italian volunteers under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> withstood a siege of nearly nine years, and the war ended in 1851 with no victor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uruguayan-civil-war.jpg" alt="Uruguayan Civil War" width="320" height="291" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: KaasterMDK · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guerra_Grande.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The new country had just under 75,000 people and two parties, the Blancos of the second president, Manuel Oribe and the Colorados of the first, Fructuoso Rivera, whose quarrel became the <b>Guerra Grande</b> in 1839.</blockquote><a href="/empire/oriental-republic-of-uruguay">Oriental Republic of Uruguay · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
